 "title": "Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of Nitrogen",
 "short": "Nitrogen",
 "lead": "Nitrogen, the main gas of the atmosphere, is prepared from liquid air and used wherever an unreactive atmosphere is wanted. The section describes how organisms fix it and the oxides it forms with oxygen, from nitrous oxide to dinitrogen pentaoxide, with the equilibrium between nitrogen dioxide and its dimer and the reactions of the oxides with water.",
 "objectives": [
  "Describe the properties, preparation, and uses of nitrogen"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>Nitrogen exhibits <span data-concept=\"oxidation-number\">oxidation states</span> ranging from 3− to 5+. Because of the stability of the N≡N <span data-concept=\"triple-bond\">triple bond</span>, it requires a great deal of energy to make compounds from molecular nitrogen. Active metals such as the <span data-concept=\"alkali-metal\">alkali metals</span> and <span data-concept=\"alkaline-earth-metal\">alkaline earth metals</span> can reduce nitrogen to form metal nitrides. Nitrogen oxides and nitrogen hydrides are also important substances.</p>",
 "notes": "The section prints no header, so its four blocks carry headers of OmniStax’s own. It keeps as printed “tetraphosphorus decoxide”, “the only known kind of biological organisms” and the full stop inside the subscript of Figure 18.38’s caption. The Sim of the nitrogen dioxide equilibrium takes ΔH = 57.20 kJ for the decomposition of dinitrogen tetraoxide from the chapter on equilibrium.",
